I agree really. I think a self-describing ASCII file format is needed.

Except I'm not one to like XML :-p

XML is a "tree" structure, flexible yes, portable, yes :-p But bulky.

I like GNUstep's property list, in theory at least, better. As said
before, I'm not an expert, and haven't worked in XML or PLists much,
but I think, at least, XML would be overkill in this situation.

PLists are much easier to edit (I've edited them by hand, and much
easier than XML). There are only 4 things to remember:

"Blah blah blah" is a string \n is newline, \\ is backslash, etc. etc.

<0123456789ABCDEF> is a hexadecimal string in Big Endian (
I think... if it isn't, it should be :-p) Basically, a text form of
binary.
True, 50% longer than base64 that XML uses, but who needs to
save space :-p

{1, 2, 3} is an array

{"Foo" =  "Bar",
 "Foo2" = "Bar2"} is a dictionary.

Well, a couple of other stuff too. But thats the basics.

Meh; whatever. I dunno any library for plists though (as opposed to
many
portable XML libraries) And I'm much to n00bish for this kind of
project...
